    {
      "id": "IejwNcA1llXYCDZuaJPc9",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 299,
      "y": 61.5,
      "width": 816.199462890625,
      "height": 350,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a0",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 704911193,
      "version": 90,
      "versionNonce": 2112685512,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1782830596068,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "\n\nBackground:\nBefore Model Foundry, model information was fragmented across multiple systems: \nmodel registries, feature tracking pipelines, compliance metadata, \nserving infrastructure, dashboards, and offline datasets.\n\nThis made it difficult for teams to get a complete view of a model. \nEngineers often had to manually jump between systems to understand ownership, \ndeployment status, inference activity, feature health, lineage, and compliance state.\n\nAs LinkedIn scaled more AI and ML use cases, this became increasingly important\nbecause model observability was no longer just a debugging tool. \nIt became part of reliability, governance, compliance, and operational efficiency.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "\n\nBackground:\nBefore Model Foundry, model information was fragmented across multiple systems: \nmodel registries, feature tracking pipelines, compliance metadata, \nserving infrastructure, dashboards, and offline datasets.\n\nThis made it difficult for teams to get a complete view of a model. \nEngineers often had to manually jump between systems to understand ownership, \ndeployment status, inference activity, feature health, lineage, and compliance state.\n\nAs LinkedIn scaled more AI and ML use cases, this became increasingly important\nbecause model observability was no longer just a debugging tool. \nIt became part of reliability, governance, compliance, and operational efficiency.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "IOaZtcaAZk0EyCz-FpRtt",
      "type": "rectangle",
      "x": 274.66015625,
      "y": 97.10546875000006,
      "width": 845.0703125,
      "height": 330.16406249999994,
