    {
      "id": "n3DomXNAgmtZvrV5Pb0hG",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 293,
      "y": 477.5,
      "width": 923.0993041992188,
      "height": 200,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a2",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1487254817,
      "version": 10,
      "versionNonce": 618204360,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1782830596068,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "The most important part of the project was not just building dashboards. \nIt was defining the right abstraction for model observability.\n\nWe needed to connect multiple dimensions: model identity, ownership, activity, \nserving status, feature dependencies, dataset freshness, compliance state, and lineage. \nOnce those signals were normalized, we could serve different users from the same foundation: \nML engineers debugging models, platform teams monitoring adoption, \ncompliance teams checking readiness, and leadership understanding AI system coverage.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "The most important part of the project was not just building dashboards. \nIt was defining the right abstraction for model observability.\n\nWe needed to connect multiple dimensions: model identity, ownership, activity, \nserving status, feature dependencies, dataset freshness, compliance state, and lineage. \nOnce those signals were normalized, we could serve different users from the same foundation: \nML engineers debugging models, platform teams monitoring adoption, \ncompliance teams checking readiness, and leadership understanding AI system coverage.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
